c  surveillance data is very rich because  not only do we observe you know a sample  in a given place at a given time but we  actually know the genotype of each  sample right which alleles are present  and which are absent which mutations are  there and which mutations are not there  and so to connect genotype to phenotype  or Fitness we need to make a more  detailed assumption and we're going to  make a very simple assumption which is  that the basic reproduction number are  here depends on the genotype linearly  right so we introduced this Delta R term  that modulates the fitness up or down  note that in the following I'm going to  be using the words Fitness and basic  reproduction number and growth rate more  or less interchangeably it's just this  number R which encodes the sort of  relative strength of a given variant as  the outcompetes other variants that are  circulating  and so this Delta R term under this  linearity assumption is just going to be  given as a linear as a sum over capital  A many alleles in our actual analysis  we're going to have about 3 000 alleles  we introduced 3 000 selection  coefficients beta one for each amino  acid  and so as a consequence each variant is  going its Fitness or R is going to be  governed by basically a linear  combination of some small number of  selection coefficients corresponding to  those selection coefficients that  correspond to those mutations that are  actually that make up that particular  variant  okay so just quickly wrapping up on this  modeling and inference technique the  fourth word in in our technique is  selection based in viral allele  selection and this word choice refers to  a set of techniques called Bayesian  variable selection which is just a way  of imposing sparsity uh in our selection  coefficients beta right so this is how  our neutrality assumption sort of gets  encoded uh at the statistical level and  so what this means uh is that we're  going to assume that most selection  coefficients b