ell  characterized by this point so for  example this d641g mutation was sort of  one of the early mutations that was  identified towards the beginning of the  pandemic as playing a role in  differential Fitness for some early  variants so it's good to see that kind  of thing recapitulated here in our  results and an interesting thing about  this Bayesian viral allele selection  approach that we're using is that we in  addition to getting you know inferences  on the actual selection coefficient beta  we also get an inter interpretable score  score called a pip or posterior  probability  um which is a score between zero and one  then encodes how much statistical  evidence there is that supports any one  of these given inferences and so that's  a useful way for us to sort of rank  these different hits but also gives us  some level some sort of notion of how  confident we should be so these top 10  hits for example all have a lot of  statistical evidence for them since they  have Pips that are very close to one at  least under our modeling assumptions  another way that we can display our  results is graphically like this so what  we have here in the left and right panel  is the structure of the S protein  and and in particular the S timer is  denoted with the yellow and blue  um you know 3D mesh frame whereas the  magenta bit is the ace2 receptor in  human cells which is sort of the main uh  viral entry point for SARS Coba 2 and  depicted in red are some of our top hits  right these are the amino acid residues  where our model thinks uh there's a lot  of selection pressure and if we zoom in  on the right we see that a lot of these  hits are actually in this interface  region between the ARP uh between the  spike trimer in particular the so-called  RBD region or receptor binding domain  and that H2 receptor so this is you know  what we would expect but it's nice to  see this kind of thing emerge from this  kind of agnostic genome-wide analysis  uh you know given that we