emic where a lot of the differential  Fitness effects that we're seeing  between different variants is driven by  immunescape and as such mutations in the  S Gene are playing a particularly  important role in this phase of the  pandemic so it's nice to see that in  this kind of figure  so there are other kinds of analysis  that you can do with this kind of setup  I'd like to just highlight one and  that's doing a vaccination rate analysis  right so everything I described up to  now is just using genomic surveillance  data but we can also try to pull in  other sources of data so in particular  one has access to vaccination rates in  different regions and how that  vaccination rate has gone up over time  so we can imagine a model that is  similar to the model I described earlier  but now it has twice as many allele  selection coefficients we still have a  vector of allele level selection  coefficients beta that are going to be  assumed to be vaccination independent  but now we're going to introduce 3000  vaccination dependent effects Alpha that  are modulated by the time local and  region local vaccination rate which here  is denoted by five and then we can try  to infer beta and Alpha from the data  and in principle this is you know this  Opera this is quite difficult right  because we went from inferring say 3 000  coefficients to six thousand so this  sort of thing is hard to do given the  size of that hypothesis space  uh but the nice thing about the kind of  Bayesian framework that we're using that  also has underlying sparsity or  neutrality assumption is that it you  know this sort of thing things Still  Remains tractable and we're still going  to you know use our parsimony assumption  to favor Simple Solutions and the  strongest evidence that emerges from  this kind of analysis  for allele level uh dependence on  vaccination rates is in a interesting  residue in the spike protein called  n501 and so it appears from our analysis  that this the vaccination actua